WEBVTT

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.580
On Monday morning, Rosa forwards Maya a guide from the local authority.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.120
It’s about running public events safely.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:11.220
“Could you turn this into a checklist for the open day?” Rosa asks.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:12.820
“I’ll need it by Friday.”

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:15.180
Maya opens the attachment.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:16.800
Thirty pages.

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:20.760
And she’s still waiting to hear how many tables the gardening club needs.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:27.980
She gives the public guide to the chat tool and asks, “Summarise this in plain language, then turn it into a checklist.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:30.880
Show me where each item comes from in the guide.”

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:33.200
A twelve-point checklist appears.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.320
Even the jargon feels manageable.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:42.380
A risk assessment, the tool explains, is where you work out what could go wrong and what you can do to reduce the risk.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:44.320
Maya looks at the checklist.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.440
It feels like something she could forward to Rosa right now.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:48.660
Would you send it?

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:51.340
Choose what Maya should do, then carry on.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.380
Maya opens the guide alongside the checklist.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.460
She starts with the safety sections.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:00.620
As she reads each requirement in the guide, she checks that it’s on her list.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:01.840
Fire exits.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:02.720
There it is.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:04.300
Emergency contacts.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:05.200
Got those.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.800
Then she reaches the first-aid section.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:13.080
For this event, the guide says a trained first-aider has to be on site for the whole day.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:15.280
Maya scans the checklist again.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:17.640
There’s no first-aid item at all.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:23.140
Every line on the checklist can look right, and the list can still leave out something important.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:28.280
Maya adds the missing item and confirms that a trained volunteer can cover the whole day.

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:36.620
On Friday, she gives Rosa the checklist with a note explaining that she used AI to draft it and checked the safety requirements against the guide.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:42.180
When you’re using a summary to make a decision, go back to the parts of the original that matter most.

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:44.860
Look for what’s missing as well as what’s there.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:46.000
Your turn.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.380
Think of a long document you deal with.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:53.260
Which part would you go back and read for yourself before acting on an AI summary?
